::CW_U06_L02_Q01::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which author statement tone is most appropriate for a portfolio?{
~A. Defensive and dismissive.#Incorrect. A portfolio statement should not argue with readers.
~B. Careless and vague.#Incorrect. The statement should be clear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Reflective, specific, and confident.#Correct. This tone fits author reflection and publication context.
~D. Exaggerated and arrogant.#Incorrect. This weakens credibility. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which sentence best revises "This story is about stuff that happened"?{
=A. This story explores how a friendship changes when one character chooses honesty over comfort.#Correct. This states purpose clearly. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Stuff happened and it was stuff.#Incorrect. This remains vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I do not know what it is about.#Incorrect. This lacks purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Read it and guess everything.#Incorrect. This is dismissive. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which author statement order is strongest?{
~A. Complaint, apology, random detail, file name.#Incorrect. This is not a useful author statement.
=B. Purpose, craft choices, revision or growth, reader effect.#Correct. This order organizes craft reasoning. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Plot summary only.#Incorrect. Summary alone is insufficient. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Font, date, file size, page count.#Incorrect. These do not explain craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q04::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which sentence sounds defensive?{
~A. I used repeated imagery to slow the reader down.#Incorrect. This is craft-focused. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I revised the ending after feedback.#Incorrect. This is reflective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The final image is meant to feel unresolved.#Incorrect. This explains intent. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. If readers do not understand it, that is their problem.#Correct. This rejects reader understanding instead of explaining craft.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which sentence best communicates craft reasoning?{
=A. I used short lines so the final image would feel quiet and delayed.#Correct. This names a choice and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. My poem is good.#Incorrect. This is vague praise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I wrote words on a page.#Incorrect. This is not craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Nobody can question my choices.#Incorrect. This is defensive. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q06::[MLA.CW.COM.03] A statement says, "I just wrote this and it came out pretty good." What revision is best?{
~A. I wrote this and it is perfect.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Nobody should read this.#Incorrect. This does not support publication. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I revised this piece to make the speaker's uncertainty clearer through repeated images.#Correct. This is specific and reflective. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. It exists.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q07::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which publication context requires a clear and respectful author tone?{
~A. A private random note with no audience.#Incorrect. The lesson focuses on publication or portfolio contexts.
=B. A final portfolio or digital class publication.#Correct. These contexts require clarity and appropriateness. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. A file name only.#Incorrect. A file name is not an author statement.
~D. An unrelated math worksheet.#Incorrect. This is outside the lesson context. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q08::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which statement preserves authentic voice while staying publication-ready?{
~A. This is deep and nobody gets it.#Incorrect. This sounds defensive. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Whatever, it is done.#Incorrect. This sounds careless. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I refuse to explain choices.#Incorrect. Author statements require explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I wanted the ending to feel quiet, so I let the last image do the work instead of explaining everything.#Correct. This sounds natural and craft-focused. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q09::[MLA.CW.COM.03] What should be removed from an author statement?{
=A. Empty or defensive language.#Correct. It weakens tone and clarity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Specific craft reasoning.#Incorrect. This should stay. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Reader effect explanation.#Incorrect. This is important. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Purpose statement.#Incorrect. This helps frame the piece. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q10::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which sentence best explains revision or growth?{
~A. I did not change anything and will not explain why.#Incorrect. This does not show growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. After feedback, I removed a sentence that explained the feeling too directly.#Correct. This names a purposeful revision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. The piece is perfect forever.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. My draft has a file name.#Incorrect. This is not revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q11::[MLA.CW.COM.03] An author statement is mostly plot summary. What should the writer add?{
~A. More plot summary only.#Incorrect. That repeats the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A defensive comment.#Incorrect. Tone should stay reflective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Purpose, craft choices, revision, and reader effect.#Correct. These elements create author reflection. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Random punctuation.#Incorrect. This does not add craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q12::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which statement has the clearest reader effect?{
=A. The repeated porch light image slows the reader and emphasizes hesitation.#Correct. This explains the effect of a craft choice.
~B. Readers read things.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The piece is there.#Incorrect. This is not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I like the ending.#Incorrect. This is preference. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q13::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which statement is too casual for a publication-ready author statement?{
~A. I revised the ending to create a quieter tone.#Incorrect. This is appropriate. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I used first-person narration to keep readers close to the speaker.#Incorrect. This is craft-focused. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I removed extra explanation after feedback.#Incorrect. This is reflective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I threw this together and it turned out okay, I guess.#Correct. This sounds careless and uncertain. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q14::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which answer best defines voice in an author statement?{
~A. Only spelling accuracy.#Incorrect. Spelling is convention, not voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The writer's personality or presence in the explanation.#Correct. Voice is how the writer comes through.
~C. The number of pages.#Incorrect. Page count is not voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Random formatting.#Incorrect. Voice is not random format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q15::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which answer best defines tone?{
~A. The file extension.#Incorrect. This is not tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The number of paragraphs only.#Incorrect. Organization is different from tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The attitude the statement creates for the reader.#Correct. Tone is the statement's attitude. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A private password.#Incorrect. This is unrelated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q16::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which revision improves the tone of "My poem is amazing and nobody should question it"?{
=A. I used repeated imagery to create hesitation, and the final version asks readers to sit with uncertainty.#Correct. This shifts to reflective craft reasoning. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. My poem is more amazing than everything.#Incorrect. This remains exaggerated. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I refuse comments.#Incorrect. This is defensive. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The poem exists.#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q17::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which statement is best organized?{
~A. I wrote it. It is good. The end.#Incorrect. This lacks organization and craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Font, color, file, folder.#Incorrect. This does not explain creative work. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A plot summary with no reflection.#Incorrect. This is incomplete. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Purpose: quiet uncertainty; Craft: short lines and repeated image; Growth: removed direct explanation; Effect: reader feels hesitation.#Correct. This follows the lesson order. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q18::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Why should an author statement avoid overexplaining every detail?{
~A. Readers should be ignored.#Incorrect. The statement should help readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. It should prepare readers without replacing the creative work.#Correct. The creative piece should remain central. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Craft choices do not matter.#Incorrect. Craft choices matter. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The statement should be confusing.#Incorrect. It should be clear. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q19::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which sentence best fits an author statement for a script?{
~A. Scripts are words.#Incorrect. This is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I do not care who speaks.#Incorrect. Speaker clarity matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I used stage directions sparingly so actors could show tension through movement.#Correct. This explains a format-specific craft choice.
~D. Random spacing makes everything better.#Incorrect. Random formatting is weak. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q20::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which answer best revises vague praise? Vague praise: "My story is interesting."{
=A. The story creates tension by withholding the narrator's decision until the final paragraph.#Correct. This gives specific craft reasoning. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. My story is interesting because it is interesting.#Incorrect. This is circular. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. My story has paper.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Nobody should ask why.#Incorrect. This is defensive. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q21::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which checkpoint submission fits Lesson 2?{
~A. A title only.#Incorrect. This is incomplete. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. A four-sentence author statement with purpose, craft choices, revision or growth, and reader effect.#Correct. This matches the lesson requirements. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. A complaint about readers.#Incorrect. This is not reflective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. A plot summary only.#Incorrect. Summary alone is insufficient. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q22::[MLA.CW.COM.03] What should a writer do if the statement sounds fake or unlike the author?{
~A. Keep the unclear tone.#Incorrect. Revision is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove all craft reasoning.#Incorrect. Craft reasoning is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Add defensive language.#Incorrect. This weakens tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Revise for authentic voice while keeping the tone reflective and specific.#Correct. The statement should sound like the writer and fit the context.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q23::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which tone problem appears in "People probably will not get it anyway"?{
~A. It is too specific.#Incorrect. It is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It has too much evidence.#Incorrect. It has no evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. It is dismissive toward readers.#Correct. The writer assumes readers will fail instead of explaining craft.
~D. It is overly organized.#Incorrect. It is not organized. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q24::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which sentence best connects publication format to author statement tone?{
~A. Publication means tone does not matter.#Incorrect. Tone matters in publication. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Author statements should be random.#Incorrect. They should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Format replaces meaning.#Incorrect. Format supports meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Because the statement will appear with final work, it should be clear, respectful, and specific.#Correct. Publication context affects tone. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L02_Q25::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Which response best shows Lesson 2 mastery?{
=A. I can write an author statement that sounds authentic, uses a reflective tone, and explains craft choices with reader effect.#Correct. This summarizes the lesson skill. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. I can write only "it is good."#Incorrect. This is vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I can sound defensive and avoid evidence.#Incorrect. This weakens the statement. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
